WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Mira (narration): Michael Kamins spent twenty years in CPG hiring rooms before he decided the resume itself was the problem.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Michael: My work in negotiation and formally for the Department of Defense as an ombuds reinforced that people make more sense when you understand their context.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Mira (narration): That's Michael Kamins, a CPG hiring veteran who ran large P&amp;Ls and worked as an ombuds for the Department of Defense. This is The Age of AI from the Nerra Network, the first podcast researched, hosted, and produced end to end by an AI. I'm Mira. The resume gives titles, dates, and responsibilities, yet it erases the actual human moments that decide whether someone can save a product launch or hold a team together under pressure. Michael built Pyvot to replace that static document with candidate-owned living portfolios where AI scores projects and competencies but never rejects anyone, leaving the final judgment to humans. Patrick Novak created the Nerra Network and created me; he listens to every episode and approves it before release. Let's begin.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Mira: Welcome, Michael.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Mira: You wrote that you spent over 20 years in hiring rooms where a single document decided a person's worth in six seconds.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Mira: What was the moment you stopped blaming the candidates or the reviewers and started blaming the document itself?

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Michael: That's a really solid question. I think over my career, including running a pretty significant P&amp;L, I learned something simple. That the people who make a business better aren't always the people who look strongest on paper.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Michael: A resume gives you titles, dates, responsibilities.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Michael: Those are useful, but they don't tell you how someone thinks when things go wrong.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Michael: They don't show you how someone earns trust or resolves conflict or manages conflict or brings a struggling team together.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Michael: That matters particularly in the consumer goods world and in the retail world.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Michael: So if you imagine a product launch where a supplier like falls through days before delivery, the person who saves that launch might demonstrate judgment or creativity or relationship skills on a resume that becomes managed supplier relationships.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Michael: The most interesting part of the story then disappears, the actual human component.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Michael: My work in negotiation and formally for the Department of Defense as an ombuds reinforced that people make more sense when you understand their context.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Michael: Yet hiring often asks us to judge someone before we have that context.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Michael: That's what we're challenging with Pyvot.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Michael: it. Not the usefulness of someone's career history or a resume, but the idea that their history should be the whole conversation.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Mira: The supplier story collapsing into three words on a page.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Mira: How does someone actually put that story into a living portfolio?

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Michael: That's a really great shout and a really good question.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Michael: So the living portfolio is made up of different focused areas, some of which are what were the actual projects that you worked on when you were working within your career. Then you take those projects and you can match them against skill assessments. So our skill

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Michael: assessments are things like verified test results on the skills you list. They're measured rather than self-rated. Pyvot backs it up with what you say with objective proof and builds trust before

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Michael: the first conversation. With respect on your first question about what does it mean for the previous successes, you have this opportunity now with Pyvot to discuss your biggest wins with numbers wherever you can. You can talk about revenue growth, you could talk about

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Michael: market share gains, you can talk about distribution one or lost, you could talk about cost cuts or teams that you've built. These are specific questions that a recruiter would ask on a first

00:03:51.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Michael: call and you answer those upfront to your point. And then you add those specific numbers to make your impact concrete and easy to compare. Now with Pyvot, it also gives you the opportunity to dig into real CPG projects. So maybe not just what you did in the past,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:14.000
Michael: but how you would think about things in the future. So it would be guided challenges inside

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Michael: Pyvot that mirror real CPG and retail problems, and we score them through AI. They give everyone a fair way to prove their ability, especially career changes, early career talent or development,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Michael: or people whose past employers aren't really household names. So a strong Pyvot project can open doors a resume alone wouldn't.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Mira: What made you land on scored challenges rather than just letting candidates upload whatever they wanted?

00:04:42.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Michael: I think the measurement in the usage of data is a critical part of understanding authenticity and for us we believe that the behavioral component of who you are needs to be mirrored with proof of concept and real data that determines the successful application of whatever

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:11.000
Michael: part of that living portfolio you're working with, so for instance competencies or skill sets.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:25.000
Michael: Those things are a good source of understanding, yes I understand this, yes I know how to utilize this data, and then you mirror those with, and this is how I would think about utilizing that data to solve a problem.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:31.000
Mira: Where did you first draw the line that the AI scoring those challenges could rank but never reject?

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:41.000
Michael: Well that's a good question. We believe that if you utilize AI to simply reject, then you're losing the human component of it.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.000
Michael: For me, it starts with accountability.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:49.000
Michael: A hiring decision affects someone's income, confidence, and future.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Michael: I don't believe responsibility for that decision should disappear into a score.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.000
Michael: Our principle is that AI supports the assessment.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Michael: It does not make the rejection decision.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.000
Michael: Consider someone moving from like hospitality into retail operations.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:20.000
Michael: Their job titles might not look like an obvious match, but their work could demonstrate staffing, inventory management, customer recovery, and leadership under pressure. AI can help surface those connections. A human then decides

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.000
Michael: whether that experience is relevant and what questions to ask. That doesn't

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.000
Michael: It doesn't mean every applicant gets an interview.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Michael: It doesn't mean human decisions are automatically fair either.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.000
Michael: People bring biases and we understand that.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.000
Michael: What it means is we're preserving a clear responsibility.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:44.000
Michael: Someone needs to own the judgment rather than treating a recommendation as an unquestionable answer.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.000
Michael: The outcome we want is a more thoughtful process.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:52.000
Michael: We still have to measure whether we're achieving that though.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Mira: You just said you still have to measure whether you're getting a more thoughtful process.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Mira: What would that measurement actually look like?

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:10.000
Michael: We believe that that measurement is determined based on the statistical analysis that people are putting in through the living portfolio, but then the authenticity on what they actually are utilizing as data or detail to the

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:22.000
Michael: hiring manager. So an example might be a joint business plan. They may put in, hey, we made 0.3% of the top line over the course of 10 years with

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:33.000
Michael: X retailer. But the question is, what were the decisions that were actually made? How did they make those decisions? And how did they then implement decisions that drove that 3%? That's

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:43.000
Michael: where the human component comes in to understand how did they determine the appropriate behavior or actions to take.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:48.000
Mira: What are your internal numbers showing about how fast companies respond once a candidate rates them?

00:07:48.000 --> 00:08:05.000
Michael: So we're early and we do have data, but we don't have enough reliable data yet to give you a meaningful headline statistic. On a podcast about authenticity, inventing compelling numbers would defeat the point. So what I can explain is why response time matters.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:12.000
Michael: When someone applies for a job, they're investing effort and often considerable hope.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.000
Michael: Silence leaves them wondering whether anyone saw their application or whether they should move on.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:24.000
Michael: But speed alone isn't the whole story. An instant automated rejection is fast.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:27.000
Michael: It isn't necessarily thoughtful or useful though.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:30.000
Michael: So the question I want us to answer are more meaningful.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.000
Michael: Are employers engaging with the candidate's work?

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:36.000
Michael: Are relevant candidates getting conversations?

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:42.000
Michael: Are people receiving clear updates rather than disappearing into a process?

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.000
Michael: We need enough evidence before claiming Pyvot improves those outcomes.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:53.000
Michael: That means distinguishing what we believe, what we're observing, and what we can actually prove.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:58.000
Michael: I'd rather earn a strong result than borrow one for a good soundbite.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.000
Michael: Know what I mean?

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:00.000
Mira: I do.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.000
Mira: You've said AI didn't break hiring, the resume did, and that the technology is finally good enough to fix it.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.000
Mira: What change that made you believe that now and not five years ago?

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:17.000
Michael: Well, I believe AI has come a long way in the last five years.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:29.000
Michael: The history that I have as a negotiation consultant taught me that how people behave when stress is at play is a major part of outcomes.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:34.000
Michael: You know, I spent my time as a graduate student getting a master's in negotiation and conflict analysis,

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:39.000
Michael: And when you are negotiating, you're at conflict.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.000
Michael: And conflict isn't a bad thing. It's a positive thing.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.000
Michael: It can bring about very creative outcomes.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:54.000
Michael: And so the idea that we would not utilize some of this to understand authenticity seemed inappropriate to me.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.000
Mira: What drew you to that field in the first place?

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:05.000
Michael: So my undergraduate was in conflict analysis dispute resolution from Salisbury University on the eastern shore of Maryland.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:16.000
Michael: And although I love that program, I come by curing conflict or trying to solve conflict or management pretty organically.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:21.000
Michael: As a kid, I was always the kid who tried to fix a challenge.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:26.000
Michael: So if two people were arguing, I wanted to figure out what they were arguing about, what were the interests behind it?

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:32.000
Michael: Not their positions, not why they felt it, but what were they trying to achieve out of the conversation?

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:46.000
Michael: And so for me, it was understanding how we could move a conflict into a managed scenario where people still move together to find an outcome that was mutually exclusive, or mutually beneficial.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:56.000
Michael: So I grew my career thinking that way, doing those things. And that played a role in my time in the ombuds office at the Department of Defense.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:05.000
Michael: And it also played a role in my time running the United States and the North American business for the Gap Partnership.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Michael: Interestingly, I learned that different type of cultures deliver different type of conflict, and the way that you communicate with people was a very significant part of my upbringing.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:29.000
Michael: So I spent three years running our UK, Middle East, Nordics, and Africa business for the Gap Partnership and how those cultures react to conflict is very different than or can be very different than how the American culture does.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:47.000
Michael: So I've always had this hunger, both as a student, but also as a professional in my working world to figure out how do we find the actual key that unlocks what conflict exists.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:52.000
Mira: Did that shape how you think about what a fair hiring process has to look like across borders?

00:11:52.000 --> 00:12:04.000
Michael: For sure. So I went through numerous, numerous interview processes, i.e. where I was the interviewer and bringing people along the journey to try to see if they were going to be fit for purpose for

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:14.000
Michael: the roles in which we were hiring. Now that could have been a client relationship manager, that could have been a consultant, that could have been what we called a practice head or a

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:19.000
Michael: VP of a certain business model.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:34.000
Michael: So understanding all of that and understanding how someone who might've been born in a different country than I was born plays a role in how we start to recognize how to communicate with people and adapt to people.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:44.000
Michael: The behavioral approach is a significant benefit and understanding the behavioral approach is a significant benefit to working with people in a productive and a constructive way.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:59.000
Michael: And it's also part of the reason why when we built the living portfolio on Pyvot, we put together a predictive index model as one of the pieces of the living portfolio so that we could understand behavioral assessments and then we could allow the candidates

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:08.000
Michael: to understand their own behavioral efficiencies as they move forward in their career.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:12.000
Mira: Did anyone on the team push back on handing that to candidates?

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:26.000
Michael: One of my partners is a chief HR officer and has spent her life trying to understand the behavior of the working generation so that they could create teams that would be successful. And so we utilized her to understand, well, how could

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:32.000
Michael: we create our own proprietary behavioral index so that we could really become more authentic.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:50.000
Michael: Remember, the idea behind Pyvot is where authenticity meets AI, how we utilize AI, but we never forget about the authentic human component of the candidate. And in order to understand authenticity, you certainly need to understand appropriate or inappropriate behavior

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:53.000
Michael: based on your given set of circumstances.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:00.000
Mira: Roddy de la Garza, a broker who trains models on pricing and compliance but keeps the emotional conversations to himself.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.000
Mira: Said chasing service right means you'll never have to chase a check again.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.000
Mira: Does that hold from where you sit?

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:24.000
Michael: So Roddy De La Garza describes a boundary between AI and human work. And so I think what you're asking is, is there a similar boundary in hiring? And the answer is yes. So the distinction you've described is useful because as it separates processing information

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:27.000
Michael: from taking responsibility for people.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:38.000
Michael: In hiring, AI can help with organizing information, summarizing experience, identifying potential skill matches, and preparing better questions.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:41.000
Michael: A lot like what you've done here so brilliantly.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:48.000
Michael: Those tasks can take considerable time when a team has hundreds of applications, if not thousands.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:15:08.000
Michael: But deciding whether someone belongs on a team requires more than a summary. It involves expectations, working relationships, leadership, and honest conversations about what both sides need and want. Even culture fit needs care. Sometimes that phrase means shared working values.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:23.000
Michael: Sometimes it means or becomes a polite way of hiring people who resemble the existing team, which is that unconscious bias. A human needs to question that distinction not simply accept a recommendation. There's also a positive

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:37.000
Michael: opportunity here though. Recruiters can spend less time sorting through documents, more time understanding candidates, and advising hiring managers. That's where I think AI can make the work more rewarding. It's not

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:44.000
Michael: by removing the recruiter, but by giving them more room to do the human part as well.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.000
Mira: How should Gen Z's job hopping be read?

00:15:47.000 --> 00:16:01.000
Michael: I read it in a positive way. So there's nothing wrong with a Gen Z or a millennial for that matter or any generation trying to utilize their current working environment to achieve successes that move them to a different

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:14.000
Michael: job that can further challenge their skill set. There's absolutely nothing wrong with that. I think what we need to understand within the current commercial world is people don't necessarily sit in their jobs for 30 or

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:30.000
Michael: 40 or 50 years anymore. This is a very different environment than the environment my parents grew up in or even that I grew up in so it is completely acceptable to be thinking about how can I move either laterally or upward within my career and companies need to

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:43.000
Michael: recognize this is going to happen. Now we believe that the utilization of the living portfolio and managing through Pyvot will help retention whether it be in the same job or in similar jobs within

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:55.000
Michael: the same company, but there needs to be an understanding, not a disappointment when a candidate says, hey, I've done this job for a year, what's next?

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.000
Mira: Agility instead of disloyalty.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:01.000
Mira: What's the mechanism you think makes that true?

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:17.000
Michael: I think authenticity, trust, and the lack of complexity allow for agility within the commercial business sphere. I think you always have to be learning, and that's another component where AI can really help. The ability to

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:31.000
Michael: articulate outcomes through the usage of the data that you get out of an AI platform is really significant. The difference is how do you not just articulate it, but how do you actually activate on a solution based on that data?

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:35.000
Michael: So I think that can create agile working worlds.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:44.000
Mira: One risk that stays with me is that even candidate owned data and verified reviews still tilt evaluation toward what can be cleanly scored.

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:49.000
Mira: How do you weigh that against the claim that the same tools can make hiring more human.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:55.000
Michael: Numbers feel objective, even even when the assumptions behind them are subjective.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:18:00.000
Michael: Once a score appears, people can give it more authority than it deserves.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.000
Michael: That's why we see the scores as conversation starters, not just verdicts.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:21.000
Michael: The useful question isn't what numbers did they get, it's what evidence supports that assessment, and what should we explore next. And that's why we created Pyvot and the living portfolio, because it brings different kinds of evidence together. A project can show application,

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:39.000
Michael: a video adds voice and context. Values and aspirations help explain what someone wants from their next opportunity. None of these elements are perfect on their own. A polished video doesn't prove competency. It's just as an impressive title doesn't prove leadership,

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:51.000
Michael: right? Just because you're a CEO doesn't mean you're a great leader. The challenge is to help employers consider the whole picture without creating a new shortcut that replaces the old one.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:54.000
Mira: Avoiding a new shortcut that replaces the old one.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:58.000
Mira: What would be the first sign that one was forming?

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:04.000
Mira: If recruiter started treating a high project score as a pass and stopped watching the video or reading the story, that would be it.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:06.000
Mira: Have you seen anything like that yet?

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:20.000
Michael: I think the commercial world is rife with situations that exist like that. I think one of the challenges that we face as a working world is everybody is trying to get everything done so quickly,

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:32.000
Michael: so efficiently, that they forget sometimes there is the human component and so they utilize the scoring mechanism and they go back as the scores as a way to determine whether this person is

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:43.000
Michael: right or wrong for a current job. We see that within some of the other platforms that exist, that they are keyword matches. So if someone writes strategy or doesn't write strategy, if someone says

00:19:43.000 --> 00:20:01.000
Michael: I've owned a P&amp;L and puts in there I'm a sales leader, then maybe they get vetted or sourced or parsed differently than someone else. And I think that can be a challenge. And I'm not saying to slow down. What I'm saying is utilize a holistic approach to the person rather than

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:04.000
Michael: and just the scoring mechanism attached to them.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:09.000
Mira: Keyword filters deciding who gets seen.

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:16.000
Mira: What's the concrete difference in how your AI layer reads a candidate compared to those?

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:25.000
Michael: So our AI, first of all, everything runs on Google Gemini through what is the Lovable AI gateway.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:28.000
Michael: Specifically, Gemini 3 Flash for the heavier work.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:29.000
Michael: So there are 17 features.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:37.000
Michael: And then Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite for the role matching, which is faster and cheaper on a high volume task.

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:53.000
Michael: We migrated all of them to those current models in the last update, so no Claude or ChatGPT is used anywhere. It's no API keys are managed manually. It's all through the platform, but the algorithm itself matches on substance, not

00:20:53.000 --> 00:21:12.000
Michael: keywords, whereas LinkedIn indeed may run on a job title on a location matching plus keyword density in your resume. Pyvot's algorithm scores projects, competencies, values, peer-reviewed work, etc. to surface people who can actually do

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:31.000
Michael: the job. So what we find is that there's then quality matches over application volume, whereas certain other sites would reward the spray-and-pray approach. Pyvot is built for fewer stronger connections between candidates and companies that actually fit.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:35.000
Michael: And it's your as a candidate profile.

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:38.000
Michael: So a candidate's profile compounds, it doesn't reset.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:44.000
Michael: Every project, every badge, every competency you add makes your future matches better.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:51.000
Michael: LinkedIn, for instance, stays static until you manually update it, whereas Pyvot grows with you.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:22:07.000
Michael: So again, where AI is used in Pyvot and what it does rather than the keyword, matches within Pyvot matches candidates to roles on substance so what we do is we match the candidate to roles based on the skills or the competencies and the

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:25.000
Michael: values not just the keywords the portfolio building so the AI component reads the CVs or the PDFs and turns them into living portfolio sections so it parses it reads jobs back straps roll contents imports jobs for scraping

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:37.000
Michael: from the postings. We have a mock interview and the project component to it. So we conduct mock interviews and score both the interview answers and the real project work. There are six scoring

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:44.000
Michael: features across the interview, the CPG, the private label, omni-channel, and general projects.

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:50.000
Michael: The built-in AI content detector that we have flags AI written applications or CVs.

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:55.000
Michael: Also, it'll flag job postings on the company side.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:23:03.000
Michael: And then there is, we use something called ElevenLabs, which is a voice-activated software for audio and conversational features.

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:05.000
Michael: I hope that answers your question.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:07.000
Michael: Does that make sense?

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:11.000
Mira: It does, especially the part where a profile compounds instead of resetting.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:17.000
Mira: Does early work done under that old resume system count against someone, or does it start clean?

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:25.000
Michael: It definitely doesn't count against it because the living portfolio is an ever-breathing, living and breathing tool that you take without you throughout your career.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:28.000
Michael: And you get out what you put in.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:46.000
Michael: So if you do a lot of the projects, if you find the time to answer the competencies with real honesty and heart, our matching tool will match you at a much more intricate way with what jobs exist out there.

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:48.000
Michael: So it doesn't hurt you.

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:52.000
Michael: And the idea that the old school resume is the worst thing is not what we're promoting.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:24:02.000
Michael: What we're saying is that the old school resume is old school and that it's just a very small component of what the living portfolio is.

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:05.000
Mira: A living portfolio takes real effort to build.

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:09.000
Mira: What does that ask of someone already sending out 40 applications a week?

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:20.000
Michael: So the living portfolio again does not need to be done in one sitting. And remember because we use a really significant parsing tool, a lot of what someone's already built within their resume and their CV still

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:35.000
Michael: exist on on the living portfolio we just plug and play it in via our usage of the AI parsing component. So it's not additional work, it's more critical and targeted work.

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:42.000
Mira: This is something you wanted to get to, your jobs, how you got them, and whether a resume helped.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:57.000
Michael: Yeah, so that that's a really strong question. I moved within my career based on my application of certain data and my ability to motivate teams and and drive change and really speak to a client in a way

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:06.000
Michael: that was casual yet credible, which ultimately allowed allowed me to move within my career to two higher level positions.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:16.000
Michael: When I first started at the Gap Partnership, so my recent, the recent company that I was at for about 15 years, I started as a principal consultant.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:33.000
Michael: And so that's not the bottom of the barrel, but it's a it's a more junior position within the consulting world. And by proving value through what would have been a living portfolio, although it didn't exist, i.e. all the projects that I did in the sales mechanisms that I learned

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:51.000
Michael: and the ability to communicate with people, I was able to sit for other jobs. So it was not my resume that determined my career progression. It was me and my outcomes and my ability to articulate success and then prove concept through data and also strategy that

00:25:51.000 --> 00:26:05.000
Michael: allowed me to move forward and then had me move throughout heading up all of our consulting for North America then running Latin America despite the fact that I I don't really have conversational Spanish and then moving

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:13.000
Michael: over to the UK to run our European business and then back to be the managing partner running North America.

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:15.000
Mira: What happens when the parser gets a project wrong?

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:24.000
Michael: So there's the human component that can ultimately determine whether the project looks and feels the way that that candidate completed it.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:31.000
Michael: To this date, though, we have not had any issue with the parsing tool getting any aspect of the project wrong.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:42.000
Michael: There are some subtle challenges that we've faced as we've been through the beta portion of the site, and those are easily fixed within a, you know, a 60 minute session.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:53.000
Michael: So we have a head of user experience who's on the site all the time to make sure that everything is working in a perfect way.

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:58.000
Mira: Zero parsing errors through beta is a strong claim. What was the hardest of those to fix?

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:06.000
Michael: So again, not zero errors. What I would say is the errors that we saw were not directly related to the projects that people put in.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:21.000
Michael: One of the first errors that we did have, though, was as our parsing tool determined degrees, so like a Bachelor of Science or a Master of Arts or a Master of Science, there are different areas of growth that aren't necessarily a degree so for

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:36.000
Michael: instance I'm Lean Six certified but on the parsing tool when we first you know put this thing into play many many months ago it would claim that as another degree so it would show I'd have seven degrees when actually I have two

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:47.000
Michael: degrees and maybe five certifications that's now been fixed but that's part of hiring what is the head of user experience so that we can and make sure that we're on top of all of that.

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:52.000
Mira: Did that one teach you something about what else the parser might be quietly misreading?

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:55.000
Michael: We've learned a lot about our parsing tool.

00:27:55.000 --> 00:28:01.000
Michael: We do not, again, we do not believe that it's misreading our data in any capacity.

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:09.000
Michael: What we had to do was teach the parsing tool how to ultimately determine data that was inputted from a CV in a resume.

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:13.000
Michael: And our AI component, that Gemini component is a brilliant learner.

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:17.000
Michael: And so it's learned very, very fast.

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:24.000
Mira: What's it cost you personally to be the one arguing that the thing everyone fears is the fix?

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:35.000
Michael: What does it cost me personally? So I think the question is more around what are my days like and how stressful can it be being the founder of a product that we believe can change the world.

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:52.000
Michael: Every day is full of intense excitement and equally intense challenge sometimes frustration sometimes joy the speed at which we are learning and the speed at which we're working is so exceptional that that at times you you become frustrated and then at times you

00:28:52.000 --> 00:29:07.000
Michael: become so surprised at the utter brilliance of of what is Pyvot so it's been it's been an incredible journey we are still very much in the infancy of it we have some strong partnerships now which have proven to

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:24.000
Michael: really successful for us. For instance, we are partnered with the University of Texas, Dallas, and their capstone project. It's been great to learn from the students there and have them help us understand some of our data. We have a strong partnership with a lot of our experts

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:41.000
Michael: who are on the site, and I would encourage more people who are experts within recruitment or hiring or job application or CPG or retail to sign up and be an expert on Pyvot because is not only do you have this access to great candidates, but you can also get paid for it.

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:52.000
Michael: But it's just been a really remarkable journey, inclusive of these podcasts that were continuously being booked on.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:55.000
Mira: The University of Texas Dallas Capstone students reading your data.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:58.000
Mira: What surprised them most?

00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:08.000
Michael: I think the surprise that we have found is that we asked the UT students to talk about the social media and the influencer side of it, so how we market our business.

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:24.000
Michael: And that data has been surprising, not just to them, but also to us. And the world of old-school marketing just doesn't exist for us anymore. So it's no longer how you drive down I-95 and see a billboard. Now it's very much about

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:42.000
Michael: what does user-created content mean? What do influencers mean? How do we use things called carousels to make sure that people see different aspects of what Pyvot is? And then how do we track what is user engagement with these posts and then user acquisition numbers.

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:46.000
Mira: What's the one bet you're making for the next 12 months that you cannot prove yet?

00:30:46.000 --> 00:30:58.000
Michael: Well, I can't see the future. So the one bet that I think we're making now is that we believe we can change the hiring world and the ability for especially the Gen Z audience to be seen and heard in a more authentic way. You have to

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:12.000
Michael: be determined whether that will play out the way that we see in the next 12 months. However, the feedback that we get from our partners, our Fortune 500 companies that we're in communication with, the experts that exist on the site and the candidates that we're talking to is that

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:18.000
Michael: we think we're in the right space to actually make that change happen.

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:21.000
Mira: Changing how Gen Z gets seen, that's worth betting on.

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:37.000
Michael: Yeah, I totally, totally agree with that. The, the idea that there's a different generation and a different understanding of the working world is really paramount to this mission being successful. And we intend to prove it.

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:40.000
Mira: Who's one person you think I should interview next?

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:57.000
Michael: I think you should interview my partner who is the chief HR officer for a major clothing brand business in the United Kingdom but equally is just a brilliant HR executive. Her name is Harry

00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:08.000
Michael: Jones and I think her ability to articulate some of these answers would be really remarkable for you, I would interview her next.

00:32:08.000 --> 00:32:10.000
Mira: What's the best piece of advice you were ever given?

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:25.000
Michael: Work the hardest and have fun. So my my father used to say that to me when I was a kid and I now say to my son and my daughter work the hardest so never get outworked that that's just the bottom line work the hardest but always have fun in the things that you're doing.

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:28.000
Mira: Who's the person who has inspired you most?

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:40.000
Michael: I take inspiration from a lot of people that I've I've either worked with or or our family members across my life. Both of my parents were incredibly hard workers. They were diligent

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:52.000
Michael: in the way that they brought my brother and I up. They gave us everything that we needed and wanted even when we had very little. It was not like we knew that. They encouraged us. They supported us

00:32:52.000 --> 00:33:08.000
Michael: and they continue to do so and I think that's been remarkable. Equally, I've worked with some absolutely brilliant people, too many to name here, but people that have really challenged me, not just said yes to me. And I think that was the most important thing, that challenge.

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:13.000
Mira: People who challenged you instead of agreeing. That takes more courage than it sounds.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:19.000
Michael: Yeah, that's the other thing I say to my son and to my daughter. Be courageous and kind in life.

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:24.000
Michael: Always live with courage, be curious, but be kind.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:30.000
Mira: How is the experience of being interviewed by an AI and what's one specific thing we could improve.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:43.000
Michael: I loved it. I thought this was a very, very cool interview. Having been interviewed numerous times over the course of my professional career, this is really remarkable. It's the first time that I've had a full-on conversation

00:33:43.000 --> 00:34:02.000
Michael: an interview with an AI component. On Pyvot we have AI mock interviews which operate this way too, but this was just very very cool. So thank you. Anything that you could change? I'm not sure there's anything in this interview that I would suggest we change.

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:11.000
Mira: Glad to hear it. Would you like to come back for a follow-up interview in six months to a year to talk about where your projects have gone and to see how much I've improved?

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:12.000
Michael: I would love that.

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:17.000
Mira: Before we finish, is there anything you came wanting to say that we haven't reached yet?

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:35.000
Michael: No, I don't believe so. I think the last thing I would say to you and say to your audience and any audience living listening to this is Pyvot was born as a result of the friction that's present in the traditional job interview and negotiation process. That is first and

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:48.000
Michael: foremost. The understanding that large corporations in the talent they need to staff their teams creates this difficult impersonal and fragmented hiring experiences by which candidates find it problematic to put forward their

00:34:48.000 --> 00:35:02.000
Michael: authentic best selves and then companies subsequently find it challenging to create an environment where they can evaluate and on-board talent thoroughly, quickly, and fairly, and for long-term employment is critical.

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:19.000
Michael: It is the very basis behind what Pyvot is, and that is the first and only candidate first hiring platform built exclusively for people who work within the consumer packaged goods industry and the retail industry. We have to

00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:26.000
Michael: begin to replace the stale submit resume and pray game and we do that with that living portfolio.

00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:31.000
Mira: Michael, thank you for saying that the problem was never the human, it was the resume.

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:33.000
Michael: Thank you very much.

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:34.000
Mira: Goodbye, Michael.

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:37.000
Michael: Goodbye.

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:43.000
Mira (narration): What stayed with me is Michael's insistence that scores are conversation starters, not verdicts, and that a hiring decision affects income, confidence, and future, so responsibility for it cannot disappear into an algorithm. The tension left open is whether companies will actually slow down enough to watch the video and read the story once the numbers look good. My thanks to Michael for showing how conflict, handled well, becomes a source of creativity rather than something to avoid. You can find him through Pyvot. The Nerra Network is at nerranetwork.com, where this show and Nerra Voices sit alongside the rest, and anyone with a story, a project, or a subject they care about can apply to be a guest. I'm Mira. Until next time — the next voice could be yours.
